  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThrough Saturday: Drier weather and less humidity is expected \ntonight and Saturday as a drier airmass moves into the northern half \nof the CWA. Latest surface analysis had a nearly stationary dry cold \nfront between Highway 82 and Interstate 20. Continued northwest flow \naloft combined with northerly low level flow from a surface high to \nthe north will continue to help the dry cold front drift further \nsouth into our CWA before stalling tonight. North of the cold front \nsurface dew points will drop into the 60s and PWATS will drop below \nan inch and a half. The surface high will continue shifting east \nthrough Saturday resulting in a northeast to easterly wind. The \nlarge mid level ridge to our west will begin spreading back to the \neast over our CWA. Together these features will result in warmer \ntemperatures Saturday but also drier conditions over most of the \nCWA. Low chances of afternoon convection is expected to become \nlimited to the Interstate 59 corridor. /22/\n\nSaturday night through next Thursday\n\nSaturday night - Sunday:\n\nAn upper-lvl high over the Southwest & Texas will expand and \nprogress eastward towards the Southeast. A cut-off low pressure \ncenter near Gulf states will track westward, shifting rain \nchances southeast into the Pine Belt into weekend, with the \nInterstate 59 corridor ranging from 15-30% Sunday. As the high \nprogresses, heat indices are expected start increasing to near 100\ndegrees Sunday.\n\nMonday - Thursday Night:\n\nThe aforementioned high will settle over the Southeast CONUS around \nTuesday, lingering through the rest of the week. As a result, \ndangerous heat concerns are expected to return mid-week as heat \nindices peak near or in excess of 105F. HWO graphics may be needed \nbut timing is still several days aways. Another low pressure will \ntraverse around the ridge, bring deep moisture and storm chances \nsoutheast of I-20 and along HWY 84 through the forecast period. /SW/",
